  "sport": "hockey",
  "title": "Depth in zones – 2-on-1 and 2-on-3",
  "description": "The area, about 20 × 20 metres (not to scale), is split into a build-up zone and an attacking zone – roughly 40/60 to 50/50. The build-up zone has a full-size net with a goalie; the attacking zone has two small nets or two cones.\n\nThe attacking team has two players in each zone. The defending team has one player in the build-up zone and three in the attacking zone. That makes a 2-on-1 in the build-up and a 2-on-3 further up. The attackers build up in their zone, pass in to a team-mate in the attacking zone and try to score in one of the small nets. The defenders try to stop the build-up and counter on the net with the goalie. After a turnover both teams may attack and defend in both zones.\n\nVariations: add jokers who play with the attacking team, or remove the zones and play freely. The full-size net at the top end is not used.",
  "coachingPoints": [
    {
      "id": "cp1",
      "text": "Give the puck carrier options forward, back and diagonally – do not stand in a line."
    },
    {
      "id": "cp2",
      "text": "Skate in depth when the puck carrier has time and can see you."
    },
    {
      "id": "cp3",
      "text": "Position yourself so you can receive and take the puck straight towards the net."
    },
    {
      "id": "cp4",
      "text": "Use the extra skater in the build-up: draw the defender before you pass."
    }
  ],
  "locale": "en",
  "difficulty": 3,
